{"step_index":62,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-12T06:44:50Z","content":"## How BE‑1 (Sensory‑Motor Engine – “Hands”) Fits with the Other Sub‑Systems  \n\nBelow is a **compact integration guide** you can give to the FE‑1, FE‑2, BE‑2, and BE‑3 teams. It shows the *contracts* (messages, RPCs, and shared storage) they need to consume or produce, and the order in which the components should be started.\n\n---  \n\n### 1️⃣ Common Runtime Foundations  \n\n| Piece | Why it’s needed | Where it lives |\n|------|----------------|----------------|\n| **ZeroMQ PUB/SUB bus** (tcp://127.0.0.1:5555‑5559) | Asynchronous, low‑latency event distribution for screen captures, hand intents, policy alerts, etc. | Started by **BE‑3** (the Boundary Hypervisor) – it creates the sockets and publishes the `heartbeat` topic. |\n| **gRPC service `Actuator`** (localhost:50051) | Synchronous command execution (click, drag, type). Guarantees request‑reply semantics and lets FE‑1/FE‑2 request precise actions. | Implemented in **BE‑1** (`actuator_server.py`). |\n| **SQLite DB `system_state.db`** | Persistent telemetry that **BE‑2** reads for episodic memory and drift detection; also used by **BE‑3** for audit logs. | File placed in the workspace root (`C:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\system_state.db`). |\n| **HMAC‑SHA‑256 token** | Simple source‑of‑truth security layer. Generated once by **BE‑3** and written to `token.bin` in the workspace. All other components must read this file and include the token in every gRPC metadata header *and* in every ZeroMQ message (as a `token` field). | Created by BE‑3 at start‑up. |\n\n> **Start‑up order** (recommended):\n> 1. **BE‑3** (creates token, starts ZeroMQ router, spawns heartbeat).  \n> 2. **BE‑2** (opens the SQLite DB, begins subscribing to the bus).  \n> 3. **BE‑1** (starts its gRPC server, connects to the bus, reads the token).  \n> 4. **FE‑2** (canvas simulator, subscribes to `sim.cursor` and can call the Actuator).  \n> 5. **FE‑1** (HUD, subscribes to `screen.events` and `hands.intent`, sends Actuator RPCs).  \n\n---  \n\n### 2️⃣ Message Topics & Payloads (protobuf definitions)\n\nAll protobuf files live in `common/proto/` and are version‑controlled together.\n\n| Topic (ZeroMQ) | Publisher | Subscriber(s) | Proto Message | Notes |\n|----------------|-----------|----------------|---------------|-------|\n| `screen.events` | **BE‑1** | FE‑1, BE‑2, BE‑3 | `ScreenEvent { uint64 ts; bytes png; repeated UIElement elements; }` | PNG is a base‑64 string; UIElement contains `id`, `rect (x,y,w,h)`, `name`, `role`. |\n| `hands.intent` | **BE‑1** | FE‑1, FE‑2 | `Intent { uint64 ts; enum Action { LOOK_CLICK = 0; LOOK_DRAG = 1; } action = 2; int32 element_id = 3; }` | Generated after gaze/hand detection and UI‑element lookup. |\n| `hands.result` | **BE‑1** | FE‑1, BE‑2, BE‑3 | `Result { uint64 ts; bool ok; string details; }` | Sent after a gRPC `Actuate` finishes. |\n| `sim.cursor` | **FE‑2** | BE‑1 (optional) | `Cursor { uint64 ts; int32 x; int32 y; string mode; }` | Virtual cursor for the canvas; BE‑1 can mirror it to the real OS if desired. |\n| `boundary.violation` | **BE‑3** | All (optional) | `Violation { uint64 ts; string component; string reason; }` | Triggered when a policy rule is broken. |\n| `hands.control` | **BE‑3** | **BE‑1** | `Control { uint64 ts; enum Cmd { RESET_CALIBRATION = 0; SHUTDOWN = 1; } cmd = 1; }` | Used for self‑healing (e.g., drift‑detected reset). |\n| `heartbeat` | **BE‑3** | All | `Heartbeat { uint64 ts; string component; string status; }` | Simple liveness ping every second. |\n\n**gRPC Service (Actuator)**  \n\n```proto\nservice Actuator {\n  rpc Execute (ActuateRequest) returns (ActuateResponse);\n}\nmessage ActuateRequest {\n  enum Type { CLICK = 0; DRAG = 1; TYPE = 2; }\n  Type type = 1;\n  int32 x = 2;          // screen coordinates\n  int32 y = 3;\n  string text = 4;      // for TYPE only\n}\nmessage ActuateResponse {\n  bool ok = 1;\n  string message = 2;\n}\n```\n\nAll RPC calls must include the HMAC token in the metadata header:\n\n```python\nmetadata = [('auth-token', token_hex)]\nstub.Execute(request, metadata=metadata)\n```\n\n---  \n\n### 3️⃣ What Each Team Needs to Do  \n\n#### **FE‑1 – Mission Control & HUD**  \n\n1. **Subscribe** to `screen.events` and overlay the latest PNG + UI element boxes on the glass‑morphic HUD.  \n2. **Subscribe** to `hands.intent` to draw a small “hand pointer” on the HUD (optional visual feedback).  \n3. When the user clicks a HUD button (e.g., *“Force Click”*), **call** the gRPC `Actuator.Execute` with the coordinates of the target UI element (use the `element_id` from the latest `screen.events`).  \n4. Show the result by listening to `hands.result` (toast or log entry).  \n5. Include the shared token in every gRPC call and ZeroMQ message you publish (if any).  \n\n#### **FE‑2 – Spatial Canvas & Simulator**  \n\n1. **Publish** its virtual cursor on `sim.cursor` (e.g., after each frame of the canvas).  \n2. **Optionally** listen to `hands.result` so the canvas can display a “real‑world click” overlay.  \n3. When a UI element in the simulated OS needs to be triggered (e.g., a virtual button), **call** the Actuator RPC just like FE‑1.  \n4. If you want the real OS cursor to follow the virtual one, **subscribe** to `hands.result` and forward the coordinates to BE‑1 via a custom `hands.control` message (`SHUTDOWN` or `MIRROR`).  \n\n#### **BE‑2 – Episodic Memory & Drift Detection**  \n\n1. **Consume** `screen.events` and `hands.intent` (and optionally `sim.cursor`). Store each as a row in the `episode` table of `system_state.db`.  \n2. Periodically compute **Δx, Δy** drift statistics from consecutive `hands.intent` → `screen.events` pairs.  \n3. If drift exceeds a configured threshold, **publish** a `hands.control` message with `RESET_CALIBRATION`.  \n4. Provide a **gRPC `Memory`** service (e.g., `GetEpisode`, `Search`) that FE‑1 can call to present a timeline in the HUD.  \n\n#### **BE‑3 – Boundary Hypervisor & Security**  \n\n1. **Generate** a random 32‑byte secret at start‑up, write it as hex to `token.bin`.  \n2. **Load** `policy.json` (list of allowed window titles, allowed actions, rate limits, etc.).  \n3. **Validate** every incoming ZeroMQ message: check token, then run policy rules.  \n4. On a policy violation, **publish** `boundary.violation` and **send** a `hands.control` `SHUTDOWN` or `RESET_CALIBRATION` command to BE‑1.  \n5. **Log** every decision to the `audit` table of `system_state.db`.  \n\n---  \n\n### 4️⃣ Quick “Run‑All” Script (for the hackathon)\n\nPlace the script in the workspace root (e.g., `run_all.bat`). It launches the components in the required order and prints a friendly status line.\n\n```bat\n@echo off\nsetlocal\n\nrem ==== paths ======================================================\nset WORKSPACE=C:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\nset BE3=%WORKSPACE%\\be3_boundary\\run_be3.ps1\nset BE2=%WORKSPACE%\\be2_memory\\run_be2.ps1\nset BE1=%WORKSPACE%\\be1_hands\\run_be1.ps1\nset FE2=%WORKSPACE%\\fe2_canvas\\run_fe2.ps1\nset FE1=%WORKSPACE%\\fe1_hud\\run_fe1.ps1\n\nrem ==== start boundary (creates token, bus) ======================\nstart \"\" powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File \"%BE3%\"\n\nrem short pause to let the bus and token appear\ntimeout /t 2 >nul\n\nrem ==== start memory (opens db, subscribes) =====================\nstart \"\" powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File \"%BE2%\"\n\nrem ==== start hands engine (gRPC server, bus subscriber) ========\nstart \"\" powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File \"%BE1%\"\n\nrem ==== start canvas (simulator) ================================\nstart \"\" powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File \"%FE2%\"\n\nrem ==== start HUD (visualisation) ==============================\nstart \"\" powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File \"%FE1%\"\n\necho All components launched.  Press any key to exit…\npause >nul\n```\n\n> *Each `run_*.ps1` script simply activates the virtual‑env in its folder, runs the Python entry‑point, and prints a short banner (e.g., “BE‑1 listening on 50051”).*  \n\n---  \n\n### 5️⃣ Checklist for the Hackathon Demo  \n\n| ✔ | Item |\n|---|------|\n| **Token** | `token.bin` exists; all components read it. |\n| **ZeroMQ bus** | `boundary` prints “Bus ready on tcp://127.0.0.1:5555‑5559”. |\n| **gRPC** | `be1` prints “Actuator server listening on 127.0.0.1:50051”. |\n| **SQLite** | `system_state.db` has tables `episode` and `audit`. |\n| **Screen ↔ Hands flow** | Move your hand over the real Notepad window → HUD shows hand pointer → automatic click opens Calculator. |\n| **Policy test** | Add “Calculator” to the *disallowed* list in `policy.json`. Try the same hand gesture → BE‑3 logs a violation and sends `SHUTDOWN`; the click does **not** happen. |\n| **Memory query** | FE‑1’s “History” button calls BE‑2’s `Memory.GetEpisode` and displays the last 5 events. |\n| **Canvas sync** (optional) | FE‑2’s virtual cursor is mirrored by BE‑1 on the real screen (demonstrates mixed‑reality). |\n\n---\n\n### 6️⃣ Next Steps for Your Team  \n\n1. **Pull the shared `common/proto/` folder** into the BE‑1 repo.  \n2. Add the **ZeroMQ** and **gRPC** client wrappers (few dozen lines) – you can copy the minimal examples from the `examples/` directory of the Antigravity SDK.  \n3. Commit the **token‑loading** utility (`load_token.py`) and make sure it’s called at module import.  \n4. Run the `run_all.bat` script; verify that each process prints its “ready” banner.  \n5. Once the basic pipeline works, iterate on the **policy rules** (BE‑3) and **drift heuristics** (BE‑2) to showcase self‑healing.  \n\nFeel free to ask for concrete code snippets for any of the interfaces above (e.g., ZeroMQ publisher in BE‑1, gRPC client in FE‑1, SQLite schema helper, etc.). Good luck with the hackathon! 🎉","thinking":"We need to respond to user request: \"How do i integrate this with the rest of the tasks like FE1, FE2, BE2, BE3\". Provide guidance, not code. Should be concise, markdown, include integration points, interfaces, data flow. Provide steps. No need to create more files. Already installed libraries. So just give explanation."}
