{"step_index":64,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-12T06:47:48Z","content":"### Next‑Step Playbook – “Hands” (BE‑1) – After the Libraries Are Ready  \n\nYou now have a **virtual environment** with all the required packages in  \n\n```\nC:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\libs_hand\n```\n\nBelow is a concise, step‑by‑step roadmap for the **remaining work** before you start writing any code.  \nEach item is a **milestone** you can tick off; the order is intentional to keep the integration smooth.\n\n---\n\n#### 1️⃣ Validate the Environment  \n\n| Action | How |\n|--------|-----|\n| Activate the venv | `C:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\libs_hand\\venv\\Scripts\\activate.bat` |\n| Check package list | `pip list` → confirm the libraries from `requirements.txt` are installed (dxcam, mss, pywinauto, pynput, keyboard, opencv‑python, mediapipe, scikit‑image, flask, structlog, pytest, pytest‑mock). |\n| Verify Python version | `python --version` → should be 3.12.x (the venv uses the system Python). |\n\n*If anything is missing, re‑run `pip install -r requirements.txt`.*\n\n---\n\n#### 2️⃣ Set Up Shared Artefacts  \n\n| Artefact | Location | Content |\n|----------|----------|---------|\n| **ZeroMQ & gRPC protobufs** | `C:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\common\\proto` | `actuator.proto`, `event.proto`, `policy.proto` (see the integration guide). |\n| **SQLite DB** | `C:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\system_state.db` | Create with the schema provided (tables `episode` and `audit`). |\n| **Security token** | `C:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\token.bin` | Empty for now – will be generated by BE‑3 later. |\n| **Policy file** | `C:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\policy.json` | Add a minimal policy (allow all) so the system starts without denials. |\n\n*Create the directories if they don’t exist (`mkdir ...`).*\n\n---\n\n#### 3️⃣ Design the Core Modules (no code yet)  \n\n| Module | Responsibility | Rough API (functions / classes) |\n|--------|----------------|---------------------------------|\n| **screen_perception** | Capture screen, enumerate UI elements (pywinauto), return a `ScreenEvent` protobuf. | `capture_screen() → bytes`, `enumerate_ui() → List[UIElement]` |\n| **os_actuator** | Wrap `pynput`, `pywin32` calls to move mouse, click, drag, type, focus windows. | `move(x, y)`, `click(x, y)`, `drag(start, end)`, `type_keys(text)`, `focus_window(handle)` |\n| **vision** | Open webcam (`opencv`), run MediaPipe hand/eye detection, output normalized screen‑space point. | `init_camera()`, `get_hand_point() → (x, y, confidence)` |\n| **calibration** | Show a 3‑point grid, collect hand points, compute homography (`scikit-image`). | `run_calibration() → HomographyMatrix` |\n| **mapper** | Convert hand point → UI element (use the scene graph from `screen_perception`). | `map_to_element(point) → UIElement` |\n| **bus** | ZeroMQ PUB/SUB wrapper (publish screen events, intents, results). | `publish(topic, protobuf)`, `subscribe(topic, callback)` |\n| **grpc_server** | Implements the `Actuator` service, validates token, calls `os_actuator`. | `start_server(host, port)` |\n| **health** | Heartbeat publisher (ZeroMQ) and graceful shutdown handling. | `heartbeat_loop()` |\n\n*Write a short one‑page **module diagram** (you can sketch it on paper or a whiteboard).*\n\n---\n\n#### 4️⃣ Define the Data Flow (sequence diagram)  \n\n1. **Video → Vision** – webcam frame → MediaPipe → hand point.  \n2. **Calibration** – apply homography → screen‑space coordinates.  \n3. **Screen Capture** – `dxcam` grabs frame → `pywinauto` builds UI graph.  \n4. **Mapping** – hand point + UI graph → target UI element ID.  \n5. **Intent Publication** – `hands.intent` (ZeroMQ).  \n6. **Actuation** – FE‑1 or BE‑1 receives intent, calls `Actuator.Execute` (gRPC).  \n7. **Result Publication** – `hands.result` (ZeroMQ) + log entry in SQLite.  \n\nDraw this diagram (a simple Mermaid chart works). It will serve as the **reference** when you start coding.\n\n---\n\n#### 5️⃣ Establish Testing Strategy  \n\n| Test Level | What to verify |\n|------------|----------------|\n| **Unit** | Each module’s pure functions (e.g., `calibration.compute_homography`). Use `pytest` + `pytest‑mock` to mock OS calls. |\n| **Integration** | End‑to‑end flow with *fake* webcam frames (static images) and *mock* UI enumeration – verify that an intent leads to a `click` call. |\n| **System** | Run the full stack (ZeroMQ bus + gRPC) on a local machine; press a key to trigger a “manual intent” and watch the cursor move. |\n| **Performance** | Measure latency from webcam frame → actuation (< 200 ms). Record in `structlog`. |\n\nCreate a **test matrix** (rows = modules, columns = test types) and assign owners if you’re working in a team.\n\n---\n\n#### 6️⃣ Create a Minimal “Hello‑World” Prototype Plan  \n\n| Step | Goal | Success Indicator |\n|------|------|--------------------|\n| **A** | Start ZeroMQ bus (BE‑3 placeholder) and publish a dummy `screen.events` every second. | HUD shows a blank screen updating. |\n| **B** | Run the `vision` module on a static webcam image → produce a hand point. | Console prints `(x=123, y=456)`. |\n| **C** | Map that point to a UI element ID using a *hard‑coded* UI graph (e.g., a single button). | `Intent` payload shows `element_id = 42`. |\n| **D** | Call the Actuator RPC with the coordinates of element 42 → move the mouse and click. | Mouse cursor visibly moves and clicks the OS window. |\n| **E** | Publish `hands.result` and write a row to SQLite. | `system_state.db` contains a new episode row. |\n\nImplement **one step at a time**; after each step, run the corresponding unit test before moving on.\n\n---\n\n#### 7️⃣ Document the Workflow  \n\nCreate a short `README.md` in the project root that contains:\n\n1. **Prerequisites** – Python 3.12, Windows 10/11, admin rights (for UI automation).  \n2. **Setup** – activate venv, run `setup_libs.bat` (the batch file you generated).  \n3. **Run** – `run_all.bat` to start the full system.  \n4. **Demo** – “wave your hand over Notepad → Calculator opens”.  \n5. **Troubleshooting** – common issues (camera not found, permission errors for UIA).  \n\nHaving this doc ready *before* you code will keep the team aligned and speed up the hackathon execution.\n\n---\n\n#### 8️⃣ Finalize the Timeline for the Hackathon  \n\n| Time | Activity |\n|------|----------|\n| **00‑01 h** | Verify venv, create DB, token file, policy file. |\n| **01‑02 h** | Sketch module diagram, write the protobuf files, generate Python stubs (`python -m grpc_tools.protoc`). |\n| **02‑04 h** | Implement **screen_perception** (capture + UI enum) – test with a static screenshot. |\n| **04‑06 h** | Implement **vision** (camera init + MediaPipe) – test on a webcam image. |\n| **06‑08 h** | Build **calibration** routine and homography; run a quick manual test. |\n| **08‑10 h** | Wire **mapper** (hand point → UI element). |\n| **10‑12 h** | Implement **os_actuator** (mouse/keyboard) and gRPC server; verify with a manual RPC call. |\n| **12‑14 h** | Connect everything to **ZeroMQ** (publish screen & intent, subscribe to result). |\n| **14‑16 h** | Add **health** heartbeat and token verification (simple HMAC check). |\n| **16‑18 h** | Write **unit tests** for each module; run them, fix failures. |\n| **18‑20 h** | Integration test of the full pipeline (hand → click). |\n| **20‑22 h** | Polish UI in **FE‑1** (simple Flask page that shows the live PNG and hand overlay). |\n| **22‑24 h** | Run a *dry‑run* of the demo, record a short screencast. |\n| **24‑30 h** | Iterate on **policy** (BE‑3) and **drift detection** (BE‑2) – showcase self‑healing. |\n| **30‑36 h** | Final polishing, documentation, create `run_all.bat`, rehearse the presentation. |\n\nAdjust the slots as needed, but keep the **first 12 h** focused on getting each independent module working; the latter half is for integration and polish.\n\n---\n\n### TL;DR – What to Do Right Now  \n\n1. **Activate** the venv and confirm all packages.  \n2. **Create** the shared artefacts (protobuf folder, SQLite DB, token file, policy.json).  \n3. **Sketch** the module diagram and data‑flow sequence diagram (paper or Mermaid).  \n4. **Write** down the concrete API signatures for each module (no implementation yet).  \n5. **Plan** the 36‑hour timeline (the table above) and assign time blocks.  \n\nOnce you have the diagrams, APIs, and artefacts in place, you’ll be ready to start coding each module with confidence, knowing exactly how they will talk to the other teams. Let me know when you’d like concrete code snippets for any of the modules or the first “hello‑world” prototype. Happy hacking! 🚀"}
