{"step_index":66,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-12T06:57:01Z","content":"## Why `activate.bat` isn’t launching automatically  \n\n`activate.bat` is a **batch file that must be run from a command‑prompt (or PowerShell) session** – it won’t start by double‑clicking it in Explorer.  \nWhen you double‑click, Windows just opens the file in Notepad.  \nYou need to **call** the script inside an already‑opened console so that the environment variables it sets stay in that shell.\n\nBelow are the exact commands you can paste into a **Command Prompt** (cmd.exe) or **PowerShell** window to activate the virtual environment that lives at  \n\n```\nC:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\libs_hand\\venv\n```\n\n---\n\n### 1️⃣ Activate from **Command Prompt (cmd.exe)**  \n\n1. Open **Command Prompt** (⌘ + R → type `cmd` → press **Enter**).  \n2. Run the *call* command (note the surrounding quotes – they protect the spaces in the path):\n\n```cmd\ncd C:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\libs_hand\ncall \"venv\\Scripts\\activate.bat\"\n```\n\n*What you should see*: the prompt changes to  \n\n```\n(venv) C:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\libs_hand>\n```\n\nThat indicates the virtual environment is active and any `python` or `pip` you invoke now will use the isolated packages.\n\n---\n\n### 2️⃣ Activate from **PowerShell**  \n\nPowerShell uses a different activation script (`Activate.ps1`).  \nIf you prefer PowerShell, do the following:\n\n```powershell\n# 1️⃣  Open PowerShell (Win + X → “Windows PowerShell”)\n# 2️⃣  Allow the script to run for this session (you only need to do this once):\nSet-ExecutionPolicy -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned -Scope Process -Force\n\n# 3️⃣  Activate the venv:\ncd \"C:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\libs_hand\"\n& \".\\venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1\"\n```\n\nYou’ll see the same `(venv)` prefix on the prompt.\n\n> **Tip:** If PowerShell complains about the script being unsigned, the `Set‑ExecutionPolicy … -Scope Process` line temporarily relaxes the policy for the current session only—no permanent change to your system.\n\n---\n\n### 3️⃣ Quick sanity check – does the venv contain the packages?  \n\nOnce the environment is active (you see `(venv)`), run:\n\n```cmd\npython -m pip list\n```\n\nor in PowerShell:\n\n```powershell\npython -m pip list\n```\n\nYou should see the libraries we installed earlier (dxcam, mss, pywinauto, etc.). If the list is empty, the venv was not created correctly – see the next troubleshooting step.\n\n---\n\n### 4️⃣ If the `venv` folder is missing or empty  \n\nIt can happen if the `python -m venv` command failed silently. Let’s recreate it:\n\n```cmd\nrem *** Delete any half‑created venv ***\nrmdir /s /q \"C:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\libs_hand\\venv\"\n\nrem *** Re‑create the venv ***\npython -m venv \"C:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\libs_hand\\venv\"\n```\n\nAfter the folder is created, repeat **Step 1** (or **Step 2** for PowerShell) to activate it, then install the requirements:\n\n```cmd\ncall \"C:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\libs_hand\\venv\\Scripts\\activate.bat\"\npython -m pip install --upgrade pip\npython -m pip install -r requirements.txt\n```\n\n(If you’re in PowerShell, replace `call …activate.bat` with the `& .\\venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1` line.)\n\n---\n\n### 5️⃣ Alternative: One‑click “bootstrap” batch file  \n\nBecause typing the commands each time can be tedious, you can create a small batch file that does the whole activation + upgrade + install sequence for you. Save the following text as `bootstrap_hands.bat` **inside the same folder** (`libs_hand`):\n\n```bat\n@echo off\nrem ==== Hands‑Engine bootstrap ====\nset \"VENV=%~dp0venv\"\n\nrem Create venv if it does not exist\nif not exist \"%VENV%\" (\n    echo Creating virtual environment...\n    python -m venv \"%VENV%\"\n)\n\nrem Activate the environment\ncall \"%VENV%\\Scripts\\activate.bat\"\n\nrem Upgrade pip and install dependencies\npython -m pip install --upgrade pip\npython -m pip install -r requirements.txt\n\necho -------------------------------------------------\necho Virtual environment ready.  To use it:\necho   call \"%VENV%\\Scripts\\activate.bat\"\necho -------------------------------------------------\npause\n```\n\nRun it by double‑clicking the file (or from a console: `bootstrap_hands.bat`).  \nIt will leave you at a **normal Command Prompt**; you still need to run the `call …activate.bat` line once the batch finishes (the batch merely sets up the environment).\n\n---\n\n### 6️⃣ What to do next (no code yet)\n\n1. **Confirm activation** – you see the `(venv)` prompt.  \n2. **Run `python -m pip list`** – verify the libraries are present.  \n3. **Check the SQLite DB** (optional) – `dir system_state.db` should show the file in the workspace root.  \n4. **Read the integration guide** you already have (the sequence diagram, protobuf contracts, etc.).  \n5. **Plan the first prototype** – decide which module you’ll implement first (e.g., `screen_perception` to capture a screenshot).  \n\nWhen you’re comfortable that the environment is active and the packages are installed, let me know which module you’d like to start building, and I can give you a *minimal starter template* (no full implementation, just the skeleton). 🚀","thinking":"We need to help user run activation script. On Windows they need to run from cmd: call the script. Perhaps need to open PowerShell? Provide steps.\n\nWe may also suggest using PowerShell: & path\\activate.ps1 if using venv. Actually venv creates activate.bat for cmd, activate.ps1 for PowerShell.\n\nUser says unable to run activate.bat. Could be that they double-clicked, need to run from command prompt. Provide instructions.\n\nAlso maybe the venv not created? But we created earlier. Should exist.\n\nProvide steps: open Command Prompt, navigate to folder, run `call \"C:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\libs_hand\\venv\\Scripts\\activate.bat\"`.\n\nAlternatively use PowerShell: `& \"C:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\libs_hand\\venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1\"` after setting execution policy.\n\nAlso we can check existence: use `dir` to confirm.\n\nWe could offer to run a command to list.\n\nWe may need to create a batch to activate and install. Already have batch file but couldn't create due to artifact restrictions. But we can give instructions.\n\nLet's respond with clear steps, plus troubleshooting.\n\n"}
