{"step_index":72,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-12T08:34:39Z","content":"Below is a **hands‑on checklist** that you can follow right now – you already have the virtual‑env and the open‑source libraries installed, so the only thing left is to create the **BE‑1 modules** and get them talking to each other.\n\n> **All paths below are absolute** (Windows) and assume the workspace root is  \n\n```\nC:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\n```\n\n---\n\n## 📂 1 Create the folder layout\n\n```bat\ncd C:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\nmkdir app\nmkdir app\\screen_perception\nmkdir app\\vision\nmkdir app\\calibration\nmkdir app\\mapper\nmkdir app\\bus\nmkdir app\\os_actuator\nmkdir app\\grpc_server\nmkdir tests\nmkdir common\nmkdir common\\proto\n```\n\nAdd an empty `__init__.py` in every new folder so Python treats them as packages:\n\n```bat\nfor /d %d in (app app\\screen_perception app\\vision app\\calibration app\\mapper app\\bus app\\os_actuator app\\grpc_server tests common common\\proto) do echo > %d\\__init__.py\n```\n\n---\n\n## 📜 2 Define the **protobuf contracts** (shared by everybody)\n\nCreate the three `.proto` files in `common\\proto`.  \n\n### 2.1 `actuator.proto`\n\n```proto\nsyntax = \"proto3\";\n\npackage actuator;\n\nservice Actuator {\n  rpc Execute (ActuateRequest) returns (ActuateResponse);\n}\n\nmessage ActuateRequest {\n  enum Type { CLICK = 0; DRAG = 1; TYPE = 2; }\n  Type type = 1;\n  int32 x = 2;\n  int32 y = 3;\n  string text = 4;   // for TYPE only\n}\n\nmessage ActuateResponse {\n  bool ok = 1;\n  string message = 2;\n}\n```\n\n### 2.2 `event.proto`\n\n```proto\nsyntax = \"proto3\";\n\npackage event;\n\nmessage UIElement {\n  int64 id = 1;\n  string name = 2;\n  string role = 3;\n  int32 x = 4;\n  int32 y = 5;\n  int32 w = 6;\n  int32 h = 7;\n}\n\nmessage ScreenEvent {\n  uint64 ts = 1;                // microseconds since epoch\n  bytes png = 2;                // PNG‑encoded screenshot\n  repeated UIElement elements = 3;\n}\n\nenum IntentAction { LOOK_CLICK = 0; LOOK_DRAG = 1; }\n\nmessage Intent {\n  uint64 ts = 1;\n  IntentAction action = 2;\n  int64 element_id = 3;\n}\n\nmessage Result {\n  uint64 ts = 1;\n  bool ok = 2;\n  string details = 3;\n}\n```\n\n### 2.3 `policy.proto` (only needed later by BE‑3, but keep it)\n\n```proto\nsyntax = \"proto3\";\n\npackage policy;\n\nmessage Policy {\n  repeated string allowed_windows = 1;\n  uint32 max_intents_per_sec = 2;\n}\n```\n\n#### Generate the Python stubs (run once, *while the venv is active*)  \n\n```bash\n(venv) > python -m pip install grpcio-tools\n(venv) > python -m grpc_tools.protoc \\\n      -I./common/proto \\\n      --python_out=./common/proto \\\n      --grpc_python_out=./common/proto \\\n      ./common/proto/actuator.proto \\\n      ./common/proto/event.proto \\\n      ./common/proto/policy.proto\n```\n\nYou’ll now have `actuator_pb2.py`, `actuator_pb2_grpc.py`, `event_pb2.py`, etc., inside `common\\proto`.\n\n---\n\n## 🖥️ 3 Implement **BE‑1 modules** (code skeletons only – you’ll fill the logic later)\n\n> **Tip** – after creating each file, run `python -m py_compile <file>` to make sure there are no syntax errors.\n\n### 3.1 ZeroMQ **bus** helper (`app\\bus\\bus.py`)\n\n```python\n# app/bus/bus.py\nimport zmq\nimport structlog\nlog = structlog.get_logger(__name__)\n\nclass Bus:\n    def __init__(self, ctx=None):\n        self.ctx = ctx or zmq.Context()\n        self.pub = self.ctx.socket(zmq.PUB)\n        self.sub = self.ctx.socket(zmq.SUB)\n\n    # ---------- publishing ----------\n    def bind_pub(self, address: str):\n        \"\"\"address like tcp://127.0.0.1:5555\"\"\"\n        self.pub.bind(address)\n\n    def publish(self, topic: str, protobuf_msg):\n        \"\"\"topic = short string, protobuf_msg = generated Message instance\"\"\"\n        self.pub.send_multipart([topic.encode(), protobuf_msg.SerializeToString()])\n\n    # ---------- subscribing ----------\n    def connect_sub(self, address: str, topics):\n        \"\"\"topics is a list of strings to subscribe to\"\"\"\n        self.sub.connect(address)\n        for t in topics:\n            self.sub.setsockopt(zmq.SUBSCRIBE, t.encode())\n\n    def recv(self):\n        topic, payload = self.sub.recv_multipart()\n        return topic.decode(), payload\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3.2 **Screen capture & UI enumeration** (`app\\screen_perception\\capture.py`)\n\n```python\n# app/screen_perception/capture.py\nimport time\nimport dxcam\nimport cv2\nimport structlog\nimport zmq\nfrom common.proto import event_pb2 as ev\nfrom pywinauto import Desktop\n\nlog = structlog.get_logger(__name__)\n\ndef _enumerate_ui():\n    elems = []\n    for w in Desktop(backend=\"uia\").windows():\n        r = w.rectangle()\n        elem = ev.UIElement(\n            id=int(w.handle),\n            name=w.window_text(),\n            role=w.friendly_class_name(),\n            x=r.left,\n            y=r.top,\n            w=r.width(),\n            h=r.height(),\n        )\n        elems.append(elem)\n    return elems\n\ndef start_publisher():\n    ctx = zmq.Context()\n    pub = ctx.socket(zmq.PUB)\n    pub.bind(\"tcp://127.0.0.1:5555\")      # **screen.events**\n    cam = dxcam.create()\n    cam.start(target_fps=30)\n\n    log.info(\"Screen publisher started\")\n    try:\n        while True:\n            frame = cam.get_latest_frame()\n            # encode to PNG (dxcam gives a NumPy array)\n            _, png = cv2.imencode('.png', frame)\n            screen_msg = ev.ScreenEvent(\n                ts=int(time.time() * 1e6),\n                png=png.tobytes(),\n                elements=_enumerate_ui(),\n            )\n            pub.send_multipart([b\"screen.events\", screen_msg.SerializeToString()])\n            time.sleep(1.0)           # 1 Hz for now (increase later)\n    finally:\n        cam.stop()\n        pub.close()\n        ctx.term()\n```\n\n**Run it** (in a new console, with the venv activated):\n\n```bash\n(venv) > python -m app.screen_perception.capture\n```\n\nYou should see `Screen publisher started` in the console.\n\n---\n\n### 3.3 **Vision stub** (`app\\vision\\hand.py`)\n\n```python\n# app/vision/hand.py\ndef get_hand_point():\n    \"\"\"\n    Placeholder that pretends a hand is always in the centre of the screen.\n    Returns (x, y, confidence) – later we’ll replace this with MediaPipe.\n    \"\"\"\n    return 960, 540, 0.99   # 1920×1080 centre, high confidence\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3.4 **Calibration utility** (`app\\calibration\\calib.py`)\n\n```python\n# app/calibration/calib.py\nimport numpy as np\nimport cv2\n\ndef compute_homography(src_pts, dst_pts):\n    \"\"\"\n    src_pts – list of (x, y) in camera coordinates\n    dst_pts – list of (x, y) in screen coordinates\n    Returns the 3×3 homography matrix (as a NumPy array).\n    \"\"\"\n    src = np.array(src_pts, dtype=np.float32)\n    dst = np.array(dst_pts, dtype=np.float32)\n    H, _ = cv2.findHomography(src, dst, method=0)\n    return H\n```\n\n*Later you’ll call this from a small UI that asks the user to look at three calibration markers.*\n\n---\n\n### 3.5 **Mapper** (`app\\mapper\\map.py`)\n\n```python\n# app/mapper/map.py\nfrom common.proto import event_pb2 as ev\n\ndef map_point_to_element(point, elements):\n    \"\"\"\n    point – (x, y) in screen coordinates (after homography)\n    elements – list of UIElement messages\n    Returns the matching UIElement or None.\n    \"\"\"\n    x, y = point\n    for elem in elements:\n        if (elem.x <= x < elem.x + elem.w) and (elem.y <= y < elem.y + elem.h):\n            return elem\n    return None\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3.6 **OS Actuator (real input)** (`app\\os_actuator\\actuator.py`)\n\n```python\n# app/os_actuator/actuator.py\nimport structlog\nfrom pynput.mouse import Controller as MouseCtrl, Button\nfrom pynput.keyboard import Controller as KeyboardCtrl, Key\nimport win32gui   # pywin32 – for window focusing\n\nlog = structlog.get_logger(__name__)\nmouse = MouseCtrl()\nkeyboard = KeyboardCtrl()\n\ndef move(x, y):\n    mouse.position = (x, y)\n    log.info(\"mouse moved\", x=x, y=y)\n\ndef click(x, y):\n    move(x, y)\n    mouse.click(Button.left, 1)\n    log.info(\"mouse click\", x=x, y=y)\n\ndef drag(start, end):\n    move(*start)\n    mouse.press(Button.left)\n    move(*end)\n    mouse.release(Button.left)\n    log.info(\"mouse drag\", start=start, end=end)\n\ndef type_keys(text):\n    for ch in text:\n        keyboard.press(ch)\n        keyboard.release(ch)\n    log.info(\"typed text\", text=text)\n\ndef focus_window(handle):\n    \"\"\"Bring the window identified by the Win32 handle to the foreground.\"\"\"\n    win32gui.SetForegroundWindow(handle)\n    log.info(\"focused window\", handle=handle)\n```\n\n> **Safety tip** – keep a hot‑key (e.g., `Esc`) that calls `keyboard.press(Key.esc)` and aborts any ongoing drag/type loop. You can add that later using the `keyboard` library.\n\n---\n\n### 3.7 **gRPC Actuator Server** (`app\\grpc_server\\server.py`)\n\n```python\n# app/grpc_server/server.py\nimport os, hmac, hashlib, structlog\nimport grpc\nfrom concurrent import futures\nfrom common.proto import actuator_pb2_grpc as act_grpc\nfrom common.proto import actuator_pb2 as act_pb\n\nlog = structlog.get_logger(__name__)\n\nTOKEN_PATH = os.path.abspath(r\"C:\\Users\\adars_6dx4mbx\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\token.bin\")\n\ndef load_token():\n    try:\n        with open(TOKEN_PATH, \"rb\") as f:\n            return f.read().strip()\n    except FileNotFoundError:\n        return b\"\"   # empty token – policy will be permissive for now\n\nTOKEN = load_token()\n\nclass ActuatorServicer(act_grpc.ActuatorServicer):\n    def Execute(self, request, context):\n        # ---- token validation (BE‑3 will fill the token later) ----\n        md = dict(context.invocation_metadata())\n        client_token = md.get(\"auth-token\", \"\").encode()\n        if TOKEN and not hmac.compare_digest(client_token, TOKEN):\n            context.abort(grpc.StatusCode.UNAUTHENTICATED, \"Bad token\")\n        # ---------------------------------------------------------\n        # Dispatch to the real OS‑actuator functions\n        from app.os_actuator.actuator import move, click, drag, type_keys\n        if request.type == act_pb.ActuateRequest.CLICK:\n            click(request.x, request.y)\n        elif request.type == act_pb.ActuateRequest.DRAG:\n            # For a demo we treat x,y as the end point; start is current cursor\n            start = (0, 0)   # placeholder – you can read mouse.position if you like\n            drag(start, (request.x, request.y))\n        elif request.type == act_pb.ActuateRequest.TYPE:\n            type_keys(request.text)\n        else:\n            log.warning(\"unknown actuation type\", typ=request.type)\n        return act_pb.ActuateResponse(ok=True, message=\"executed\")\n        # ---------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef serve():\n    server = grpc.server(futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=4))\n    act_grpc.add_ActuatorServicer_to_server(ActuatorServicer(), server)\n    server.add_insecure_port(\"[::]:50051\")\n    server.start()\n    log.info(\"Actuator gRPC server listening on 50051\")\n    server.wait_for_termination()\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    serve()\n```\n\nRun it (new console, venv active):\n\n```bash\n(venv) > python -m app.grpc_server.server\n```\n\nYou should see: `Actuator gRPC server listening on 50051`.\n\n---\n\n### 3.8 **Coordinator / pipeline** (`run_pipeline.py` – sits at the workspace root)\n\n```python\n# run_pipeline.py\nimport threading, time, structlog\nfrom app.vision.hand import get_hand_point\nfrom app.mapper.map import map_point_to_element\nfrom app.bus.bus import Bus\nfrom common.proto import event_pb2 as ev\n\nlog = structlog.get_logger(__name__)\n\ndef intent_worker():\n    bus = Bus()\n    # Subscribe to the screen events published by capture.py\n    bus.connect_sub(\"tcp://127.0.0.1:5555\", [\"screen.events\"])\n    while True:\n        topic, payload = bus.recv()\n        if topic != \"screen.events\":\n            continue\n        screen_evt = ev.ScreenEvent()\n        screen_evt.ParseFromString(payload)\n\n        # Get a hand point (static for now)\n        x, y, conf = get_hand_point()\n        # Map to a UI element from the current screen event\n        element = map_point_to_element((x, y), screen_evt.elements)\n        if element:\n            intent = ev.Intent(\n                ts=int(time.time() * 1e6),\n                action=ev.Intent.LOOK_CLICK,\n                element_id=element.id,\n            )\n            bus.publish(\"hands.intent\", intent)\n            log.info(\"Intent published\", element=element.name, x=x, y=y)\n        else:\n            log.info(\"No UI element under hand point\")\n        time.sleep(0.2)   # avoid busy loop\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    # 1️⃣ launch the screen publisher in a thread\n    threading.Thread(target=__import__(\"app.screen_perception.capture\").capture.start_publisher,\n                    daemon=True).start()\n    # 2️⃣ start the intent worker (main thread)\n    intent_worker()\n```\n\nRun the coordinator:\n\n```bash\n(venv) > python run_pipeline.py\n```\n\nYou should see log entries like:\n\n```\nINFO  Screen publisher started\nINFO  Intent published element=\"Calculator\" x=960 y=540\n```\n\nAt this point you have **the whole BE‑1 data‑flow working**:\n\n```\ndxcam screen → UI graph → (static) hand point → mapper → Intent (ZeroMQ)\n```\n\nNo real OS actuation yet – that will be triggered when another component (FE‑1, FE‑2, or a manual test) calls the gRPC `Actuator.Execute` RPC.\n\n---\n\n## ✅  Immediate “Next‑Ready‑to‑Run” Checklist\n\n| ✅ | Done? | What to do next |\n|---|-------|----------------|\n| 1 | **Protobuf files** created and Python stubs generated. | Verify `common\\proto\\actuator_pb2.py` etc. exist. |\n| 2 | **Screen capture** (`app\\screen_perception\\capture.py`) runs and publishes `screen.events`. | Open a second console, subscribe with `python - <<PY … PY` to confirm payload size. |\n| 3 | **Vision stub** (`app\\vision\\hand.py`) returns a centre point. | Nothing else needed now. |\n| 4 | **Mapper** (`app\\mapper\\map.py`) can locate an element from the UI list. | Test by running the coordinator (see step 8). |\n| 5 | **ZeroMQ bus** helper (`app\\bus\\bus.py`) available. | Already imported by the scripts. |\n| 6 | **gRPC server** (`app\\grpc_server\\server.py`) starts (stub only). | Run it and keep the console open – later you’ll call it from FE‑1. |\n| 7 | **Coordinator** (`run_pipeline.py`) ties capture → intent together. | Run it; you should see the intent logs. |\n| 8 | **SQLite DB** (optional for now) – you can create it later when BE‑2 starts ingesting events. | No action required now. |\n\nIf every line in the table shows **✅**, you are **ready to start the next BE‑1 phases**:\n\n1. **Replace the vision stub** with a real MediaPipe pipeline (hand landmarks).  \n2. **Add calibration**: show a 3‑point grid, collect hand points, compute the homography, then apply it inside `get_hand_point()` so the hand coordinates map correctly to screen space.  \n3. **Wire the Actuator RPC**: from the intent worker, call the gRPC service (instead of just logging) to perform a real click.  \n4. **Log results** (`hands.result`) into the SQLite DB for BE‑2 to consume.  \n\nLet me know which of those next phases you’d like the **first concrete code snippet for** (e.g., MediaPipe hand detection, homography UI, or a client that calls the Actuator RPC). I’ll provide a ready‑to‑paste implementation. 🚀","thinking":"The user wants help with \"the elements in the list\" as they already have libraries activated. Likely they want to see the step-by-step tasks for BE-1 and maybe code snippets for each. Provide guidance and maybe code skeletons for each element. Provide clear tasks and code examples.\n\nWe should produce concise markdown with tasks, code skeletons, and how to run each. No need to use tools."}
